The Macro System (Configuration)

The heart of the control system is the macro file. It defines which voice commands trigger which actions. The system is designed for maximum flexibility: In case of syntax errors within the triggers, the system outputs 2 consecutive pings as a warning.
The Processing Process
Before a command is interpreted, it passes through the file speech-replace.en. Here, words are converted into characters (e.g., the spoken word "period" or "dot" is converted to the character ".").
Structure of a Macro
A command consists of four central sections:

   1. Trigger (Activation word):
   * Upper and lower case are ignored.
      * Placeholders: Use .* as a wildcard (e.g., turn on .*ight.*, in case the system misinterprets "light" as "night").
      * Grouping: Multiple words can be grouped in parentheses with | (Or): (turn|on) on (light|lamp).
      * not possible is (table lamp) better:  
      	"(on|turn.*) on (table|light) (light|lamp)" - it reacts to "table lamp on" or "light on" or "turn on table lamp" or "on light" or "turn on (the) table lamp"
      	the number of words must match. Unfamiliar words are ignored or filtered out, so they can be included without issue. 
      	The important thing is that the keywords are present within a spoken sentence.
      	If you say, "Please turn the light on right now; otherwise, I can't see anything," that is also correct, as it includes the words "light" and "on."
      	Word:
      	  1 or 1      1    2  or 2       2  or 3
      	"(on|turn.*) on (table|light) (light|lamp)"
      	
   2. Terminal_Command:
   * Standard terminal commands or scripts are entered here.
      * Parameters such as spoken numbers can be passed directly (e.g., for temperature control via MQTT).
   3. Intern_Command:
   * spell: Activates spelling mode (automatically removes spaces).
      * repeat: Repeats commands or scripts multiple times.
      * calc: Performs mathematical calculations with recognized numbers.
      * puncture: Activates automatic punctuation (can be overridden with priority via {nopuncture} in speech-replace.en).
   4. TTS (Text-to-Speech / Voice Output):
   * Determines what the PC replies after executing the command. Variables can be used here for confirmation.

Special Modes: Direct & Dictation

* Direct Mode (Without Wake-Word): If another file (e.g., screengrid.en) is specified in intern_command, MiceSt switches to this mode. Commands are now executed directly without the word "Computer". The mode ends as soon as the wake-word is spoken again.
* Dictation Mode: If a file with the ending -write.en (e.g., dictationmode-write.en) is called, everything spoken is written directly as text. Note: Currently, only lowercase letters are possible here.

Internal Variables

* {last_spoken_text}: Passes everything said after the trigger (ideal for "Write the text...").
* {last_spoken_number}: Extracts the last number from a sentence (integers or decimals) and converts it into a numerical value.
* {last_spoken_numeral}: Captures single digits from 0 to 9 (important for dictation mode or PIN entries).

Important Practical Tips

* Hierarchy: Short commands must always be placed after longer, similar commands (e.g., 1. "Close window now", 2. "Close window").
* Timing: While a command is being executed, no new triggers are accepted. If the system does not understand an input, the window remains active for 10 seconds before the wake-word is needed again.
* Management: Use the terminal tool edit-macro.py to conveniently edit triggers.

